# The distributions this project publishes a preset for.
#
# One line per preset:
#
#   preset;distro;release;variant;package
#
# The middle three fields are in the same field order and with the same
# separator the upstream's own index uses, because every line here is looked up
# in that index.
#
# The preset name is what a user writes in values.yaml, the three fields after it
# are what identify the build on images.linuxcontainers.org, and the last is the
# image repository the preset publishes into, under this project's registry
# prefix. None of the four is derived from another: `voidlinux;current` reads as
# `void-current` to a person and publishes into `stateful-pods-void`, and nothing
# should have to know either of those rules twice.
#
# The package is per distribution and variant, and the tag is per release, which
# is how a distribution names its own images. Two releases of one distribution
# therefore share a package and are told apart by their tags - so the retention
# job's unit of work is a release, not a package, and it is written that way even
# though every package holds exactly one release today.
#
# The variant is in the package rather than in the tag because two variants of one
# release carry the same upstream serial: debian trixie cloud and debian trixie
# default are both built as 20260830_05:17. A tag naming only the release and the
# serial would therefore name two different root filesystems, and the build's
# "this dated tag is already published, leave it alone" path would quietly keep
# whichever was published first. The tag could have carried the variant instead,
# but the retention planner reads a release out of a tag as the text before the
# first dash, so a release segment with a dash in it is a rewrite of the planner
# and its suite rather than a line here.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for what exists. The build matrix, the
# daily bump, the retention job and `hack/check-presets.sh` all read it, so a
# preset added here and nowhere else is built, published, kept up to date and
# checked against the shipped catalog without any of them being edited.
#
# The variant is `cloud` wherever the upstream publishes one, because the chart's
# default provisioning backend is cloud-init and an image without it fails a
# default install. An earlier revision of this file said the opposite - that the
# `cloud` variants "carry cloud-init that this chart does not drive" - and that
# clause is what stopped being true.
#
# Void is the exception, and not by preference: the upstream publishes only
# `default` and `musl` for it. Its preset therefore carries no cloud-init and
# serves the `native` backend only.
#
# Alpine also publishes `tinycloud`, which is tiny-cloud rather than cloud-init.
# It is a different implementation with a different configuration surface, so it
# would not answer the same values the other three do.
#
# Adding a release is a line. Removing one is a line and a catalog entry, and
# stops the daily bump proposing it - it does not withdraw what is already
# published, because a machine's source has to stay resolvable, and it leaves
# that release's rolling tag behind in a package other releases may still use.
debian-trixie;debian;trixie;cloud;debian-cloud
ubuntu-noble;ubuntu;noble;cloud;ubuntu-cloud
alpine-3.24;alpine;3.24;cloud;alpine-cloud
void-current;voidlinux;current;default;void
